    "text": "To determine the modulo \\(6\\) remainder of the sum \\(1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + \\dots + 98 + 99 + 100\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the sum of the first 100 positive integers:**\n\n   The sum of the first \\(n\\) positive integers is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n   For \\(n = 100\\):\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{100 \\cdot 101}{2} = 5050\n   \\]\n\n2. **Find the remainder when 5050 is divided by 6:**\n\n   We need to compute \\(5050 \\mod 6\\). To do this, we can simplify the problem by finding the remainders of the individual digits of 5050 when divided by 6.\n\n   First, let's break down 5050 into its digits:\n   \\[\n   5050 = 5 \\cdot 10^3 + 0 \\cdot 10^2 + 5 \\cdot 10^1 + 0 \\cdot 10^0\n   \\]\n\n   Now, find the remainders of each digit when divided by 6:\n   \\[\n   5 \\mod 6 = 5\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   0 \\mod 6 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5 \\mod 6 = 5\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   0 \\mod 6 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Sum these remainders:\n   \\[\n   5 + 0 + 5 + 0 = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the remainder when 10 is divided by 6:**\n\n   \\[\n   10 \\div 6 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 4\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   10 \\mod 6 = 4\n   \\]\n\nThus, the modulo \\(6\\) remainder of the sum \\(1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + \\dots + 98 + 99 + 100\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
